    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d838">

                <pb facs="1069" n="1057" sameAs="frus:frus1946v08/pg1057"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">892.00/8–1046: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Siam</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Stanton</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Bangkok</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-08-10T09:00:00+07:00">August 10, 1946—9
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 11—4:53 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>928. 1. In view of reports that an uprising was in progress in Angkor-Siemreap
                    area of Cambodia, Military and Naval Attachés<anchor n="61" xml:id="d838-f1"/>
                    flew up there yesterday. They were informed by French military and civil
                    officials whom they contacted that 4[00] to 500 “rebels” composed of Cambodians,
                    Annamites, Japanese and Siamese attacked and occupied town of Siemreap 3 days
                    ago but withdrew after 2 hours. Colonel Vance and Commander Gardes saw evidences
                    of the attack but damage to property appeared light. French rushed troops to
                    area from Saigon and appeared expect further trouble.</p>
                <p>2. French officials charged that force which attacked Siemreap and which they
                    claimed was still in vicinity was under Siamese leadership. Attachés were not in
                    position to determine veracity of this accusation. They may return to area next
                    week by arrangement French authorities but in any event will endeavor follow
                    developments and carefully evaluate all information and reports.</p>
                <p>Repeated Saigon as 13.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stanton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d838-f1" n="61" xml:id="d838-f1-content">
                <p>Col. Reginald F. C. Vance and Comdr. Alfred W. Gardes, respectively.</p>
            </note>
